. What you won’t find is any added sugar. Regularly consuming excess added sugar intake is linked to an increased risk of developing chronic inflammation. Though many people can enjoy added sugar in moderation, it tends to find its way into our routine in surprising ways. You’ll often see it in marinades, granolas, dressings, packaged snacks and of course sweetened beverages and most desserts. While it’s not realistic (or necessary) for most people to avoid added sugar entirely, it may be helpful to follow the American Heart Association’s guidelines of limiting added sugar to 9 teaspoons (36 grams) for men and 6 teaspoons (25 grams) for women. While this plan skips added sugar, it includes a wide array of proteins, fruits, vegetables, whole grains and healthy fats to align with the Mediterranean and anti-inflammatory diets. You’ll find three different calorie levels to choose from and meal-prep tips at the beginning of each week. Check it out!","type":"text"},{"allowAutoplacedAds":false,"components":[{"data":{"descriptors":[{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d0","key":"_f0","label":{"text":" Day ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d1","key":"_f1","label":{"text":" 1 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d2","key":"_f2","label":{"text":" 2 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d3","key":"_f3","label":{"text":" 3 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d4","key":"_f4","label":{"text":" 4 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d5","key":"_f5","label":{"text":" 5 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d6","key":"_f6","label":{"text":" 6 ","type":"formatted_text"}},{"dataType":"text","identifier":"_d7","key":"_f7","label":{"text":" 7 ","type":"formatted_text"}}],"records":[{"_f0":{"inlineTextStyles":[{"range":{"length":6.0,"start":0.0},"textStyle":"_anf-ts-101"},{"range":{"length":6.0,"start":0.0},"textStyle":"_anf-ts-101"}],"text":